<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">İsmet İnönü</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ismet Inonu, İsmet Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">İsmet İnönü was a Turkish general and statesman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>'s closest ally. He won the battles of İnönü against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in 1921, from which he took his surname, negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> in 1923, was prime minister for most of 1923–1937 and succeeded Atatürk as president from 1938 to 1950, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and allowing multi-party elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu.jpg" alt="İsmet İnönü" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Official photo of President. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inonu_Ismet_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
